{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 29, :comments ["<b>If you do with us.</b> They examined the precise wording and order of the covenant that they were cutting with him, that it was for the sake of the goodness, and that is his saying <b>if you do with us harm, as</b> etc. — meaning: the oath shall be on account of the goodness that you received from us. And in this there is a reason for the division of the verses in this order, for it would have been proper to make a break at the word ״כַּאֲשֶׁר״ etc., and the words ״אִם תַּעֲשֵׂה עִמָּנוּ רָעָה״ should have been joined to the preceding verse, which concludes ״בְּרִית עִמָּךְ״ — there they would have found their place to mention the covenant." "And according to our words it is precisely examined, for he intends to examine the order of the oath, that it should be for the sake of the goodness. And their saying <b>we did not touch you</b> etc. — since the acknowledgment of the kindness they did, that it was for Yitzhak's benefit, was only after acknowledgment of this detail, for you will find that the statutes of kings who extend a hand and aid to the inhabitants of their land, to acquire wealth, are for two reasons: the first, so that if they need to borrow from them, they will have a household and wealth to help at once; the second, in order that their kingdom be exalted, for it is elevated when many wealthy and prominent men multiply in it, since this is among the necessities of the kingdom. And for either of the two reasons one could say that Avimelekh's kindness to Yitzhak was intended for his own enjoyment. Therefore he was precise to say <b>as we did not touch you</b> from our days — even with a touch, which is something that involves no monetary loss but is merely something that circulates in the eyes (i.e., a trifle). And with this, truly, we have done with you only good — meaning, stripping away the reasons that pertain to themselves, and he justified that the intention was specifically to do him good. And from the side that you may say the intention was for the elevation of the kingdom — behold, we sent you away, you and your possessions, and that is his saying <b>and we sent you away in peace</b> — meaning, and with your possessions. You have seen to know that our intention in doing good with you was not for the necessities of the kingdom; and from now on there was no reality to justify that good, for we intended for his benefit — except after they sent him away the matters were recognized as true. And in this they provided an additional reason as well for why they sent him away, for this is a thing that establishes the benefit of enjoyment that validates the oath. And all this is included in the general statement of their saying ״רָאוֹ רָאִינוּ״." "<b>And I saw</b> the statement of our Rabbis of blessed memory, who said (Genesis Rabbah 64), and this is their language: this teaches that they did not do complete goodness with him — end quote. And at first glance their words are exceedingly far-fetched, for they came to appease him and to show him extraordinary affection, and they would tell him a diminution of the good? But according to what I have written, that their intention was so that the oath would be for the sake of goodness, their words, may their memory be for blessing, shine like the sun. For they said that the goodness they did with him was not unequivocally for his benefit, but for the desired purpose they hoped for from him, which is the covenant that they ask of him — and this is correct and hammering like a flint (כְּפַטִּישׁ יְפוֹצֵץ)." "And his saying <b>you are now blessed of HaShem</b> — meaning, that no evil befell him through their sending him away, for behold he is blessed just as he was before, and the intention in this is that the good on whose account you swore to us was not nullified." "<b>And know</b> that even though the Rivash wrote that it is not called 'for the sake of goodness' unless the goodness existed at the time of the oath, the same judgment applies if at the time of the oath it is clear that he swore on account of a goodness he had already done for him — this is comparable to charging the goodness against him as a debt, and he swears in consideration of the forgiveness of the debt." "<b>Further</b>, it intends to give a reason why they are not sending him back to the land, for he is blessed of HaShem — on the contrary, to a greater degree than before. For until now his influx came through the intermediary of the city, which is under the governance of supernal officers; whereas now he has a place to himself, and his influx was not through an intermediary but rather blessed of HaShem." "<b>Further</b>, it intends to preclude the claim of contradiction of the favor, for the good that reached him was not from them but from HaShem. And the proof: behold, today, in the place where he is, he is blessed with every good measure. Therefore they said <b>you are now blessed of HaShem</b> — meaning: now, after we did you a favor through the available intermediary, the source of blessings was found to flow upon you in abundance of good. And in this manner they imposed the oath with full force, so that there is no possibility of release; but without this, he could have released it. And even though we say in the chapter HaSholeiḥ (Gittin 46a), according to the opinion of the Sages who disagree with Rabbi Yehudah, that the reason Israel did not strike the Gibeonites is because of the sanctification of the Name — and Rashi explained that the nations should not say that the children of Israel violated their oath — behold that although there does exist a ground for release, yet since it would constitute a desecration of the Name, one does not release it — this is not from the fundamental principle of the Torah but a safeguard. And furthermore, I have already written at length in the laws of vows regarding this specific point, that it applies specifically to an oath that is publicly known, like that of the Gibeonites, as I closely examined there from the words of Rashi." "<b>And further.</b> Even if it were the case that the Sages hold that one may not release any oath of a non-Jew, there is still the opinion of Rabbi Yehudah, who does not distinguish between a non-Jew and an Israelite, for the nations know that there exists release for an oath and there is no desecration of the Name involved. Therefore Avimelech was shrewd to impose upon him an oath that has no release even according to the opinion of Rabbi Yehudah."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :notes {2 "The closing phrase וּכְפַטִּישׁ יְפוֹצֵץ ('and like a hammer it shatters') is borrowed from Jeremiah 23:29, used idiomatically to mean 'decisively correct.'", 4 "The acronym הריב״ש refers to Rabbi Yitzhak ben Sheshet (Ribash), 14th-century Spanish halakhic authority."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:44:05.375369362Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}